==Old English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|ang|gem-pro|*kattōn}}. Cognate with {{etyl|ofs|-}} {{m|ofs|katte}}, {{etyl|osx|-}} {{m|osx|katta}}, {{etyl|dum|-}} {{m|dum|katte}} ({{etyl|nl|-}} {{m|nl|kat}}), {{etyl|goh|-}} {{m|goh|kazza}} ({{etyl|de|-}} [[Katze]]), {{etyl|non|-}} {{m|non|ketta}} ({{etyl|sv|-}} {{m|sv|katta}}). The word existed in the Germanic languages in a masculine gender also, represented in Old English by {{m|ang|catt}}. The word appears to be related to {{etyl|LL.|-}} {{m|la|cattus}} as well as to similar words in the Slavic and Celtic languages, but the ultimate source is uncertain.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈkɑtːe/|lang=ang}}

